        <property name="plainText">
         <string>Indicator Description:
The basic principle of RSI (Relative Strength Index) is that in a normal market, the forces of bulls and bears must reach a balance for the price to remain stable. RSI measures the ratio of the average total upward price movement to the average total price movement over a fixed period.
Trading Rules:
1.RSI values follow a normal distribution within the range of 0–100. When the 6-day RSI exceeds 80, the market is considered overbought; if an M-top pattern appears, market risk is elevated. When the 6-day RSI falls below 20, the market is considered oversold; if a W-bottom pattern appears, market opportunity increases.
2.The default reference periods for RSI are typically 6, 12, and 24 days. A longer period produces a more trend-following indicator (slow RSI), while a shorter period produces a more sensitive indicator (fast RSI). When the fast RSI crosses above the slow RSI from below, opportunities increase; when the fast RSI crosses below the slow RSI from above, risk increases.</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
